razors say that in the blade stealth 13  they have  the world's first gaming ultrabook and  while i've  really enjoyed my time with this tiny  little beast  i'm worried that in their pursuit of  trying to become the jack of all trades  they might end up as the master very few  now it's important to keep in mind that  it's not really fair to put this thing  up against  your traditional gaming laptop it's not  advertised as  a gaming laptop it's an ultrabook it's  meant to be  thin and light and compact and discreet  and  stealthy i guess it's not trying to be  the all-powerful bulky huge  heavy gaming laptops that you see it's  meant to fit  in more of its own middle ground and for  the most part i think it does that  really well the first thing that you're  likely to notice that for  gaming device this thing is pretty small  it's just over half an inch thick  and weighs only 1.4  kilograms the power supply and the cable  weighs just over 600 grams  so you're packing quite a lot of power  in about two kilos now if this was a  parallel universe where we could travel  this is going to be an incredible little  device  to keep on you at all times to throw in  your backpack because it is so small  and light and compact yet very very  powerful  if you take in you know it's square  inches to  the amount of power that it puts out  it's extremely impressive  so packed within this discreet package  you have an 11th gen  intel core i7 1165 g7 quad-core cpu  it features the 13.3 inch 120 hertz  1080p display  its own dedicated nvidia geforce gtx  1650  ti max q gpu 16 gigabytes of dual core  ram  and a 512 gigabyte nvme ssd  now that is something i want to go into  a little bit later  and then for your input outputs you have  two type a usb  3.1 ports two thunderbolt 4 usbc  ports both of which can be used to power  the device if you want to  switch either side and a 3.5 millimeter  headphone  and microphone jack it's hd webcam also  features windows hello  so that's less time on fiddly login  screens and more time jumping right into  your games  the touchpad is microsoft precision  glass  now i don't know how much you're going  to use the touchpad for gaming  i can definitely say that as far as  touchpads go this is one that i  ate the least i've tried it out for  strategy games like crusader kings  played boulder's gate with it  and it works pretty well i don't think  any point was i like  oh yeah i'd rather this over a mouse the  keyboard also felt great for pretty much  any game that i was playing on it  features a full-sized left-hand side  shift key which is really nice as well  and it has just enough feedback for  every keystroke  not so loud that you're going to annoy  anyone in the office just loud enough  just enough tactile feel can actually  make you feel like you're pressing the  key  again this fits in a nice middle ground  that it felt good for gaming it felt  good for word processing editing videos  daily use it take a little bit me to get  used to  but for a couple days it does feel  like a really great keyboard especially  for a device this size  when it comes to the speakers that was  also an area that i was extremely  impressed with  it has probably some of the best  speakers i've heard on a laptop and  definitely the best speakers i've heard  on an  ultrabook it uses four stereo speakers  with thx's spatial audio and all that  together creates something that's  really good for when you're consuming  media and which i assume  a lot of people would use this for but  surprisingly good  when it comes to playing games  gun firing call of duty explosions in  doom  all sounded again surprisingly good  coming out of a device this thin  now what i'm sure you want to know is  how the thing performs playing all these  games  after all calls itself the world's first  gaming ultrabook  does it live up to that g word in its  title  well while playing doom eternal on high  i was averaging  about 70 to 80 frames per second  sometimes maxing out at 112 frames  i never felt a single dip or real low  point  or stutter when it came to the frame  rate and on the 120 hertz display  it was of course looking fantastic  oldest gate 3 however is a strange run  because  it is obviously in early access so some  of the performance bugs might need to be  squashed but that being said i was still  getting an extremely  steady 60 to 70 frames a second  star wars squadrons was a little more  hit and miss i was able to get  70-ish frames on high but i was feeling  a fair few stutters when the game was  getting a little cluttered  or action-packed a lot of enemies on the  screen  a lot of debris i did start to feel the  slowdown now when i really started to  feel a performance hit it was when i was  playing  call of duty modern warfare now this  game is plastered all over  the pages for the razer blade stealth on  the razer website  so i was assuming that i was going to  get some pretty good numbers  but it didn't perform quite as well as i  wanted it to  and for the most part i was getting  anywhere from 60 to 65 frames per second  in multiplayer lobbies but again even on  the low to medium settings  when the action gets really intense the  frames do  start to crash but for modern warfare in  particular it suffers from  really good frames from up to 90 fps  when it's a little quieter  and a little less action-packed all the  way down to the 20s when things get a  bit more intense  massive number of explosions and things  like white phosphorus would  absolutely tank the frame rate even on  low to medium settings  but for the other 90 of the time it ran  really well  and again being able to play games like  this on a device this small  is still a great experience  now one of my main gripes with this  device is also connected to call of duty  modern warfare  and that is the size of the ssd with  only 512 gigabytes  that's not a lot when you consider the  asking price of these things anything  from a grand and a half 